<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
    "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>

    <!-- ── wBond's OWN bundle identity (WB-E M4, R-h8-9 applied a third time) ─────
         A different application, and macOS will not tolerate it pretending otherwise: two bundles
         sharing one CFBundleIdentifier collide in Launch Services, in the Dock, in the quarantine
         database and in the login-item list, and the symptoms of that are all remote from the cause.

         THE THREE-PLACE TRAP: CFBundleIdentifier must match BUNDLE_ID in bundleForWBondMacOS.sh AND
         the entitlements argument to codesign. Nothing derives one from another. The bundle script
         reads the identifier back OUT of this file and checks it against its own, which is the one
         place the three can be compared — see its own note. -->

    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>com.circuitRF.wBond</string>

    <!-- The binary inside Contents/MacOS/. The ASSEMBLY is still shared with circuitRF on purpose
         (WB40: RfCore's InternalsVisibleTo names that assembly, and the standalone binary IS src/Ui
         with a different Main) - but the published HOST is renamed per app by CircuitRF.Ui.csproj's
         CrfRenameApphost target, so this must match EXECUTABLE_NAME in bundleForWBondMacOS.sh. -->
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>wBond</string>

    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>wBond</string>

    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>wBond</string>

    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>

    <key>NSPrincipalClass</key>
    <string>NSApplication</string>

    <!-- PLACEHOLDER: bundleFor*MacOS.sh stamps these two keys from the repo-root VERSION file, the
         one place circuitRF's version is written. Editing them here changes nothing about a built
         bundle. -->
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>0.1.0</string>

    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>0.1.0</string>

    <!-- .NET 10 requires macOS 13 (Ventura) or later. -->
    <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
    <string>13.0</string>

    <!-- WHAT THIS KEY IS NOT: it has nothing to do with THIS app's own quarantine, and setting it
         does not suppress any Gatekeeper dialog. It governs whether files the app CREATES OR
         DOWNLOADS get the com.apple.quarantine attribute — the flag a browser or a download manager
         opts into. circuitRF is not that kind of app, so it is explicitly false (which is also the
         default).

         The comment here used to claim it "suppresses the quarantine 'damaged app' dialog for
         ad-hoc signed builds". It never did, and believing it costs real time: an ad-hoc signature
         is one Gatekeeper cannot trust at all, and NO plist key changes that. Signing with a
         Developer ID certificate and notarising is the only thing that does. -->
    <key>LSFileQuarantineEnabled</key>
    <false/>

    <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
    <string>wBondIcon</string>

    <!-- .wBond is IMPORTED here and EXPORTED by circuitRF's Info.plist (R-wbe-7, the R-h8-10 rule
         applied to a type that was not declared to macOS at all until now). Two applications must
         not both claim to DEFINE one type — that is what Launch Services cannot arbitrate; circuitRF
         ships the definition because the format is circuitRF's, and this states that wBond
         understands it. Both then open a double-clicked .wBond. -->
    <key>UTImportedTypeDeclarations</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>UTTypeIdentifier</key>
            <string>com.circuitrf.wbond</string>
            <key>UTTypeDescription</key>
            <string>wBond Design</string>
            <key>UTTypeConformsTo</key>
            <array>
                <string>public.json</string>
                <string>public.content</string>
            </array>
            <key>UTTypeTagSpecification</key>
            <dict>
                <key>public.filename-extension</key>
                <array>
                    <string>wBond</string>
                    <string>wbond</string>
                </array>
            </dict>
        </dict>
    </array>

    <!-- Document types this app can open. .crfw and .charm are deliberately ABSENT: there is no
         workspace in this build and no harmonicaRF either, so claiming those types would put an
         application that cannot open one in the "Open With" list for every workspace on the machine.
         wBond's role here is EDITOR — the standalone is the app that owns a .wBond. -->
    <!-- ── macOS privacy: the file-access consent prompts (2026-08-27) ───────────
         macOS gates ~/Documents, ~/Desktop, ~/Downloads, removable volumes and network volumes
         behind per-folder privacy grants (TCC), and shows a consent prompt the FIRST time an app
         touches one. THESE STRINGS ARE THE PROMPT'S EXPLANATORY TEXT. Apple documents them as
         required for these services.

         WHY THEY ARE NOT OPTIONAL, and why the failure is the expensive kind: with no string
         declared, the prompt is at best unexplained and at worst never shown — and a request that
         is never prompted is simply DENIED. The app then reports "Access to the path ... is
         denied" for a file whose own permissions are perfectly normal, and wBond may not appear
         in System Settings > Privacy & Security > Files and Folders AT ALL, because that list is
         populated by apps that have actually asked. The user is then told to enable a setting
         that is not there. Nothing logs, nothing crashes; the app simply cannot open the user's
         own documents.

         This was reached the long way round in 2026-08 from a real report of exactly that error
         (src/Ui/RESOLVED.md). CircuitRF.Diagnostics' FileAccessDiagnostics turns the raw
         UnauthorizedAccessException into a message naming the privacy setting; these keys are the
         other half — they are what makes the setting exist to be named.

         Keep the wording specific about WHAT is being opened. "would like to access your files"
         is what users decline. -->

    <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>wBond needs access to your Documents folder to open and save your bond-wire designs and the Touchstone files they export.</string>

    <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>wBond needs access to your Desktop to open and save your bond-wire designs and the Touchstone files they export.</string>

    <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>wBond needs access to your Downloads folder to open and save your bond-wire designs and the Touchstone files they export.</string>

    <key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
    <string>wBond needs access to your removable volumes to open and save your bond-wire designs and the Touchstone files they export.</string>

    <key>NSNetworkVolumesUsageDescription</key>
    <string>wBond needs access to your network volumes to open and save your bond-wire designs and the Touchstone files they export.</string>

    <key>CFBundleDocumentTypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>CFBundleTypeName</key>
            <string>wBond Design</string>
            <key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
            <string>Editor</string>
            <key>LSItemContentTypes</key>
            <array>
                <string>com.circuitrf.wbond</string>
            </array>
            <key>CFBundleTypeExtensions</key>
            <array>
                <string>wBond</string>
                <string>wbond</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>

</dict>
</plist>
